</div>

<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Is DaVinci Resolve really 100% free with no watermark?</summary>
<p>Yes. Blackmagic Design offers the full Resolve editor for free with no watermark, no time limit, and no feature nags. The paid "Studio" version (₹23,000 one-time) adds AI tools, noise reduction, and some advanced features — but the free version is genuinely capable of professional work. I edited my first 50+ paid projects on the free version before upgrading.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Can I make money editing with free software?</summary>
<p>Absolutely. DaVinci Resolve Free is used by professional colorists and editors worldwide. The free version's color grading toolset alone is better than what many paid editors offer. Your clients care about the final result, not what software you used to create it.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is CapCut safe to use?</summary>
<p>CapCut is owned by ByteDance (TikTok). It collects usage analytics and some metadata. For personal social media content, this is generally fine. For corporate or client work with sensitive material, some companies prohibit ByteDance software. Use your judgment based on your client's requirements.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Why not just use Premiere Pro's free trial?</summary>
<p>Adobe's free trial is 7 days. After that, you're paying ~₹1,700/month. That's ₹20,000+ per year. For a beginner, that money is better spent on a RAM upgrade or an external SSD. Start with Resolve Free, build your skills, and consider Premiere only when you have paying clients who specifically require Adobe workflows.</p>
</details>

